# Performance

Redocly runs inside the JVM on GraalJS, so no Node.js is needed. By default GraalJS interprets the JavaScript,
which is fine for small descriptions and slow for large ones. The first line each goal logs names the engine in
use:

```
[INFO] Redocly 2.57.0 - JavaScript engine: GraalJS interpreter (add org.graalvm.polyglot:js-isolate-linux-amd64-community to the plugin dependencies for faster runs)
```

Enable the native isolate
-------------------------

For a 2–3× speed-up, add the native isolate named in that log line as a dependency **of the plugin** (not of
the project):

```xml
<plugin>
  <groupId>net.mwalser</groupId>
  <artifactId>openapi-toolkit-maven-plugin</artifactId>
  <version>${project.version}</version>
  <dependencies>
    <dependency>
      <groupId>org.graalvm.polyglot</groupId>
      <artifactId>js-isolate-linux-amd64-community</artifactId>
      <version>${project.properties.getProperty("graalvm.version")}</version>
      <type>pom</type>
    </dependency>
  </dependencies>
</plugin>
```

Platforms: `linux-amd64`, `linux-aarch64`, `darwin-aarch64`, `windows-amd64`; JDK 21 to 25. On a JDK the isolate
does not support, the build logs a warning that names the cause and continues on the interpreter.

Benchmarks
----------

These measurements compare cold lint runs on four cores:

| Description | Size | Node | Interpreter | Native isolate |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Petstore | 17 KB | 0.06 s | 1.1 s | 0.4 s |
| Twilio API | 1.9 MB | 1.1 s | 18 s | 7 s |
| GitHub REST API | 12.9 MB | 6 s | 90 s | 44 s |

build-docs renders the reference with React inside the JVM, which costs more than linting:

| Description | Node | Interpreter | Native isolate |
|---|---|---|---|
| Petstore | 0.4 s | 2.3 s | 1.6 s |
| Twilio API | 2.1 s | 21 s | 12 s |
| GitHub REST API | 19 s | 223 s | 110 s |

The GitHub page is 71 MB, as with the Redocly CLI, and rendering it took close to 3 GB of memory. On the
interpreter that is JVM heap: give Maven more (`MAVEN_OPTS=-Xmx4g`) for descriptions of that size. The native
isolate manages its own heap.

Engine selection
----------------

Which engine runs is decided once per build:

| Engine | When | Cost |
|---|---|---|
| GraalJS with runtime compilation | Maven runs on a GraalVM JDK whose version matches the embedded GraalJS (25) | none |
| GraalJS native isolate | the isolate dependency above is present | about 60 MB of downloads, 140 MB extracted to `~/.cache/org.graalvm.polyglot/` |
| GraalJS interpreter | otherwise (any JDK 21+) | none |

The engine and the Redocly bundle are initialized once per Maven JVM and reused by every goal and module, so the
warm-up (about 4 s for the interpreter, 1.4 s for the isolate) is paid once per build. build-docs evaluates a
second bundle with Redoc and React when it first runs (about 4 s for the interpreter, 2.5 s for the isolate).

Isolate troubleshooting
-----------------------

- If the isolate dependency is present but cannot start, the build continues on the interpreter and a warning
  names the cause. Check the JDK version, and that the artifact matches your platform and the GraalJS version
  in the plugin's [POM](https://github.com/mwalser/openapi-toolkit-maven-plugin/blob/main/pom.xml).
- The isolate extracts roughly 140 MB per extraction into `~/.cache/org.graalvm.polyglot/`, and the cache can
  hold several. If the home directory is read-only, set `-Dpolyglot.engine.userResourceCache=<writable-directory>`
  in `.mvn/jvm.config`.
